  "text": "An A B test, two versions of a product shown to users split at random, can be lost before anyone sees it. The hypothesis, the metric, who gets split, how many users, and when it stops are all settled before launch, and a mistake in any of them is hard to undo later. That's the design round, fourteen questions. Eleven are worked through on simulated experiments, and three brief ones come at the end. Begin with the whole plan.\n\nQuestion one. How would you design an A B test for a new feature? Write the plan before anything runs. A hypothesis: the new onboarding raises seven-day activation. One primary metric, and a guardrail or two, like page latency, that must not get worse. Randomize by user. Size the test for the smallest lift worth finding, at eighty percent power and five percent alpha. Run it for whole weeks. Then test the difference, and check the guardrails. Question two. How do you choose the primary metric? It should be tied to what the feature is for, sensitive enough to move with the effect you expect, and measurable inside the test window. Raw clicks are easy to inflate. Ninety-day retention won't arrive before the test ends. Seven-day activation passes all three. And agree on it before launch, so no one switches metrics after seeing the results. Next, what exactly gets split.",
